1.3.544:
Yes; that you’ll come back speedily.
JUPITER
I will;

2.1.596:
In what way?
SOSIA
In no degree, I say, is this more strange to you than to myself; nor, so may the Deities love me, did I at first credit Sosia—me myself, until that Sosia, I myself, made me to believe me myself. In order did he relate everything, as each thing came to pass, when we sojourned with the enemy;

3.3.957:
Very opportunely are you come.
SOSIA
Has peace been made then between you two? But since I see you in good humour, I’m delighted, and it is a pleasure to myself. And so does it seem becoming for a trusty servant to conduct himself;

4.1.1009:
(Enter AMPHITRYON.) AMPHITRYON
(to himself.)
Naucrates, whom I wanted to find, was not on board ship;

5.1.1097:
The same alarm has scared ourselves, affrighted with horror;
Prithee, relieve me quickly from this, since you have kept me long enough in suspense. What happened then?
BROMIA

3.3.729:
I have been the foot.
ARGYRIPPUS
Why, neither head nor foot of your talking is visible;

2.2.259:
I remember it.
EUCLIO
But I understand in what fashion you, of your class, are wont to equivocate;

2.4.325:
he’s in the habit of going out to cook.
ANTHRAX
You, you three-lettered fellow;

4.3.624:
(Enter EUCLIO, from his house.) EUCLIO
(to himself.)
It wasn’t for nothing that the raven was just now croaking on my left hand;

4.10.777:
I’m satisfied. Come, then, say what you wish.
LYCONIDES
If you know me but imperfectly, of what family I’m born: Megadorus here is my uncle; Antimachus was my father; my name is Lyconides;

5.1.830:
That which you confessed just now to be in your box.
STROBILUS
I’ faith, I’m in the habit of talking nonsense;

1.2.158:
Enough of these stories.
LYDUS
This youth is lost to shame;

4.10.982:
What did he say?
CHRYSALUS
He uttered not a word: crying, he listened in silence to what I was saying; in silence he wrote down on his tablets; these sealed he gave to me;

2.4.289:
But your wife and your son have promised her to me.
STALINO
I know it;

5.2.875:
(Enter OLYMPIO, in great alarm, from the house of ALCESIMUS.) OLYMPIO
(bawling aloud.)
Neither where to fly to, nor where to conceal myself, nor how to hide this disgrace, do I know; so much have my master and myself been supereminently disgraced at these nuptials of ours. I’m now so ashamed, and now so afraid, and so ridiculous are we both. But, a simpleton, I’m now doing what’s new to me: I’m ashamed at that which has never shamed me before. (To the AUDIENCE.) Lend me your attention, while I repeat my exploits; it’s worth your while to catch them with your ears;

2.1.509:
Is that quite resolved upon by you in your heart?
MELAENIS
Why, in fact, I’m thinking about something else;

4.2.769:
But I feel compassion for her.
LAMPADISCUS
This I think ought to be done;

2.3.317:
May Jupiter and the Deities confound you.
CURCULIO
I’m quite undone; I can hardly see;

2.3.328:
You’ve undone me.
CURCULIO
I can find something, if you’ll give me your attention. After, at your request, I had set out, I arrived in Caria;

3.1.453:
Aye, and I can tell you still more, if you like.
LYCO
No; I don’t want it. Follow me this way. I’ll pay you that, on account of which you came;

4.2.494:
I remember, and I shall deliver her to you on warranty.
CURCULIO
And am I to take anything on warranty from a Procurer, people who have nothing of their own except a tongue only;

5.3.679:
(Enter CAPPADOX, in haste.) CAPPADOX
(to himself.)
Those who say it’s bad for bankers to be trusted, utter nonsense;

1.1.19:
What do you wish me to tell you but that which is fact?
EPIDICUS
To answer to these things fairly;

1.1.69:
Why so?
THESPRIO
I’ll tell you;

2.1.173:
I have some regard for my son.
APAECIDES
But, i’ faith, the wife whom you buried I thought you had felt some respect for;

2.2.181:
(Enter EPIDICUS, from the house of CHAERIBULUS, softly crossing the stage.) EPIDICUS
(at the door, as he enters.)
Hist! hist! be silent, and have good courage; with a fair omen have I come out of doors, the bird upon the left hand. (Pointing to his head.) I’ve got a sharp knife, with which to embowel the old man’s purse;

2.2.289:
Cleverly thought of!
EPIDICUS
By which means you’ll keep him away from her;

2.2.293:
Epidicus, receive my thanks. But I’ll attend to this with all care.
EPIDICUS
I’ll find him and bring him here to you, to whom the music-girl belongs;

3.2.359:
Not a bad precaution!
EPIDICUS
The cautious man’s now taken in himself;

3.4.489:
How, cut me up?
A CAPTAIN
Such is my suspicion;

3.4.499:
What business have you, then, at my house?
MUSIC-GIRL
You shall hear;

1.2.110:
(Enter MENAECHMUS of Epidamnus, from his house.) MENAECHMUS of Epidamnus.
(speaking at the door to his WIFE within.)
Unless you were worthless, unless you were foolish, unless you were stark wild and an idiot, that which you see is disagreable to your husband, you would deem to be so to yourself as well. Moreover, if after this day you do any such thing to me, I’ll force you, a divorced woman, turned out of my doors to go visit your father. For as often as I wish to go out of the house, you are detaining me, calling me back, asking me questions;

2.1.267:
Why are you apprehensive?
MENAECHMUS SOSICLES
Lest you should cause me some damnable mishap in Epidamnus. You are a great admirer of the women, Messenio, and I’m a passionate man, of an unmanageable disposition;

4.2.571:
(Enter MENAECHMUS of Epidamnus.) MENAECHMUS of Epidamnus.
(to himself.)
How we do practise a custom here that is very foolish and extremely troublesome, and how even those who are the most worthy and great do follow this habit: all wish their dependants to be many in number;

1.2.111:
(Enter ACANTHIO, at a distance, in haste.) ACANTHIO
(to himself.)
With your utmost power and might always try and endeavour that your younger master may by your aid be preserved. Come then, Acanthio, away with weariness from you; take care and be on your guard against sloth. At the same time put an end to this panting; troth, I can hardly fetch my breath;

2.3.419:
He said that he would take her back, if she didn’t suit.
DEMIPHO
There’s no need of that;

3.1.504:
Why have you bought me?
LYSIMACHUS
What, I, bought you? For you to do what you are bidden;

3.2.544:
(Enter DEMIPHO.) DEMIPHO
(to himself.)
At last I’ve managed to ruin myself;

3.4.633:
What could I do?
CHARINUS
What could you do, do you ask me? You should have enquired, and asked who he was or whence he was, of what lineage;

4.1.673:
What burden?
SYRA
Fourscore years and four, and to that are added servitude, sweat, and thirst;

5.4.983:
I confess it; undoubtedly I’ve acted wrong.
EUTYCHUS
What, do you speak, you hobgoblin? You ought at this time of life to have done with these guilty practices. Just as the seasons of the year, so different lines of conduct befit different ages;

2.18.542:
What now?
SCELEDRUS
Pardon my ignorance and my folly; now, at length, I know that I am half-witted, blind, and thoughtless;

3.4.834:
Why so?
LUCRIO
Because, in fact, I only sipped;

4.8.1368:
I can hardly refrain from bidding you to stay.
PALAESTRIO
Take you care how you do that. They may say that you are a liar and not truthful, that you have no honor;

4.8.1372:
Be off;
I’ll be content then, whatever happens.
PALAESTRIO

3.1.532:
(Enter a BANKER, at the end of the stage.) A BANKER
(to himself.)
I never knew any year worse for money upon interest, than this year has turned out to me. From morning even until night, I spend my time in the Forum;

3.1.562:
Whither are you betaking yourself?
TRANIO
I’m going no whither. (Aside.) For sure, I am a wretch, a rascal, one born with all the Gods my foes! He’ll now be accosting me in the old man’s presence. Assuredly, I am a wretched man;

3.1.624:
Really, don’t think much of that;
it’s a trifle, in fact.
TRANIO

3.2.723:
Tell me then, these usual goings-on, what are they?
SIMO
The way that you yourselves proceed. But, Tranio, to say the truth, according as men are, it so befits you to humour them;

5.1.1093:
What then, if I go fetch some men?
TRANIO
It ought to have been done already;

5.2.1122:
(Enter CALLIDAMATES, at a distance.) CALLIDAMATES
(to himself.)
When I had buried all drowsiness, and slept off the debauch, Philolaches told me that his father had arrived here from abroad; in what a way too his servant had imposed upon the man on his arrival;

1.1.24:
I’ faith, it’s with reason then you are so pale.
TOXILUS
I’ve been wounded in the battles of Venus;

4.4.599:
Unless it’s troublesome, he’s desirous to make a few enquiries of her.
SAGARISTIO
By all means;

4.6.693:
Why are you in such haste?
SAGARISTIO
My business is of that nature; the letters that have been entrusted me, I want to deliver;

5.1.753:
(Enter TOXILUS, from the house.) TOXILUS
(to himself.)
The foe subdued, the citizens safe, the state in tranquillity, peace fully ratified, the war finished, our affairs prospering, the army and the garrisons untouched;

5.2.1039:
O my fellow-countryman, greetings to you!
AGORASTOCLES
And you, troth, whoever you are;

5.4.1234:
Are even my own dogs barking at me?
AGORASTOCLES
Then, troth, do you caress me;

5.4.1261:
We are both your daughters;
we both embrace you.
AGORASTOCLES

1.1.60:
Well, that’s to-morrow;
my ruin is near at hand, unless I have some help in you.
PSEUDOLUS

1.2.159:
But this one is blunted on the edge.
BALLIO
Let it be so, then. And so are you yourselves with stripes;

1.5.526:
What battle?
PSEUDOLUS
Why, with the procurer your neighbour; by means of stratagem and artful tricks, I’l cleverly bamboozle the procurer out of this music-girl, with whom your son is so desperately in love;

4.7.1175:
Bravo! By my troth, you did come quickly;
how very swift of foot this man is.
BALLIO

1.2.151:
How so?
SCEPARNIO
Why, because, after dinner, I fancy, they yesterday washed themselves clean;

3.6.848:
Who is now protecting them there?
TRACHALIO
Some old gentleman, I don’t know who, a neighbour of the Temple of Venus—he gave very kind assistance;

4.3.949:
Why, what reason is there that it should matter to me?
TRACHALIO
So it is;

4.3.953:
I do give you my word; I’ll be true to you, whoever you are.
TRACHALIO
Listen. I saw a person commit a theft;

4.3.964:
And in what manner it was lost.
GRIPUS
But I know in what manner it was found;

5.1.1281:
(Enter LABRAX, at a distance.) LABRAX
(to himself.)
What other mortal being is there living this day more wretched than myself, whom before the commissioned judges Plesidippus has just now cast? Palaestra has just been taken from me by award. I’m ruined outright. But I do believe that Procurers were procreated for mere sport;

1.3.263:
Well now, are you coming or not?
GELASIMUS
Well, be off home;

2.2.358:
The beginning pleases me, about the couches.
PINACIUM
Others, you chop the billets; others, you clean the fish which the fisherman has brought;

5.5.751:
Whack go my savings. I’m done for;
freedom has abandoned this person of mine.
STEPHANIUM

1.2.133:
Ought I not to have paid him the money?
MEGARONIDES
You ought not to have paid him; nor ought you either to have bought anything of or sold anything to him;

4.2.871:
Young man, what do you want? hat is it you wish? Why are you knocking at this door?
A SHARPER.
Eh! old gentleman;

4.2.889:
What is this name of yours, young man?
A SHARPER.
Hush, that’s my name;

1.2.159:
How’s that?
ASTAPHIUM
I’ll tell the reason;

2.2.259:
Health to you.
STRATILAX
Enow of thy health have I; I care nought for’t, I’ve got no health;

2.6.482:
(Enter STRATOPHANES, at a distance, followed by a SERVANT and several FEMALE SLAVES.) STRATOPHANES
(to the AUDIENCE.)
Don’t you be expecting, Spectators, that I should recount my combats; with my hands in battle I’m wont to recount them, and not in words. I know that many a soldier have told lies;

3.1.645:
(Enter STRABAX.) STRABAX
(to himself.)
One morning a short time since my father ordered me to go hence, to deal out the mast for food for the oxen. After I got there a person arrived at the farmhouse (so it pleased the Gods), who was owing money to my father, who had formerly purchased some Tarentine sheep of my father;
